# Distributing the desktop app

`dude build` produces installers under `src-tauri/target/release/bundle/`
(`.dmg`/`.app` on macOS, `.msi`/`.exe` on Windows, `.deb`/`.AppImage`/`.rpm`
on Linux). Building the binary is only half the story: to hand it to someone
else without friction you also need to **sign** (and on macOS **notarize**)
it. This page explains why, and how.

## The symptom: "{{projectName}} is damaged and can't be opened"

If a colleague downloads your `.dmg` and macOS says the app *is damaged and
can't be opened — move it to the Bin*, **the app is not corrupt.** This is
Gatekeeper rejecting an app that is not signed with an Apple Developer ID and
not notarized.

What happens:

1. By default `dude build` produces an **ad-hoc signed** app (no Developer ID).
   That is enough to run on the machine that built it, but nowhere else.
2. When the file is downloaded / AirDropped / sent over the internet, macOS
   tags it with the `com.apple.quarantine` attribute.
3. On the recipient's Mac, Gatekeeper sees "quarantined + not a valid
   Developer ID + not notarized" and blocks it. On Apple Silicon this shows up
   as the misleading *"damaged"* message.

### Unblock a build without signing (dev / internal only)

The recipient can strip the quarantine attribute manually:

```bash
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/{{projectName}}.app
```

(The usual right-click → **Open** does **not** clear the *"damaged"* message —
`xattr` is required.) This is fine for a teammate testing a build; it is **not**
an acceptable experience for real distribution. For that, sign and notarize.

## macOS: Developer ID signing + notarization

Requires a paid [Apple Developer](https://developer.apple.com/programs/)
account. Tauri signs and notarizes during `dude build` when you provide the
credentials.

1. Add your Developer ID and (recommended) hardened-runtime entitlements to
   `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`:

   ```json
   {
     "bundle": {
       "macOS": {
         "signingIdentity": "Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID)",
         "entitlements": "entitlements.plist"
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. Export the notarization credentials as environment variables before
   building (use an app-specific password **or** an App Store Connect API key,
   not your real Apple ID password):

   ```bash
   export APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID)"
   # notarization — either the Apple ID trio…
   export APPLE_ID="you@example.com"
   export APPLE_PASSWORD="app-specific-password"
   export APPLE_TEAM_ID="TEAMID"
   # …or an API key:
   # export APPLE_API_ISSUER=… APPLE_API_KEY=… APPLE_API_KEY_PATH=…

   dude build
   ```

Tauri signs the bundle, submits it to Apple for notarization, and staples the
ticket to the `.dmg`. The result opens with a double-click on any Mac.

Full reference: [Tauri — macOS code signing](https://tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).

## Windows: signing to avoid SmartScreen

Unsigned Windows installers trigger a **SmartScreen** "unknown publisher"
warning. Sign the installer with a code-signing certificate (an OV certificate
still warns until reputation builds; an **EV** certificate is trusted
immediately). Configure `bundle.windows.certificateThumbprint` (or use the
Azure Trusted Signing / `signCommand` options) in `tauri.conf.json`. See
[Tauri — Windows code signing](https://tauri.app/distribute/sign/windows/).

## Linux

`.AppImage` and `.deb`/`.rpm` don't require signing to run, but sign your
`.deb`/`.rpm` (GPG) if you publish through a repository so package managers can
verify integrity.

## CI

Signing secrets should live in your CI provider's secret store, exported as the
same environment variables shown above, so release builds are signed
automatically. Never commit certificates or credentials to the repo.
